{"vid":"FuExlxdoTcY","source":"FuExlxdoTcY.en-GB.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":0,"txt":"From BBC Learning English."},{"t":2,"txt":"This is Learning English from the News, our podcast about the news headlines."},{"t":6.8,"txt":"In this programme:"},{"t":7.8,"txt":"Devastating flash floods on the Nepal Tibet border"},{"t":11,"txt":"with a death toll of more than 1000 people."},{"t":18.9,"txt":"Hello, I'm Phil."},{"t":20.2,"txt":"And I'm Georgie."},{"t":21.6,"txt":"In this programme, we look at one big news story"},{"t":24.9,"txt":"and the vocabulary in the headlines that will help you understand it."},{"t":28.6,"txt":"You can find all the vocabulary and headlines from this episode,"},{"t":32,"txt":"as well as a worksheet on our website, BBC Learning English dot com"},{"t":36.4,"txt":"So let's hear more about this story."},{"t":44.4,"txt":"More than 1000 people have been confirmed dead"},{"t":47.3,"txt":"after last week's flash floods on the border of Nepal and Tibet."},{"t":51.3,"txt":"According to Nepal's disaster agency."},{"t":54,"txt":"Almost 4000 people are still missing."},{"t":57.2,"txt":"Rescuers are trying to reach hundreds of workers who've been trapped"},{"t":60.6,"txt":"in a large network of tunnels at hydropower sites."},{"t":64.7,"txt":"Nepalese Prime Minister Birendra Shah said on Monday"},{"t":67.8,"txt":"that 279 people had been rescued from the sites."},{"t":72,"txt":"A key cause of the deadly floods is thought to be a glacier collapse"},{"t":75.7,"txt":"in the Himalayas."},{"t":77,"txt":"A glacier is a large area of dense ice."},{"t":80.7,"txt":"Let's have our first headline."},{"t":82.5,"txt":"This one is from Sky News, an international news organization."},{"t":86.5,"txt":"Seven days after the disaster caused by the flash floods in Nepal,"},{"t":90.6,"txt":"it's 'clearly not over'."},{"t":93.2,"txt":"That headline, again from Sky News and international news organization,"},{"t":97.1,"txt":"seven days after the disaster caused by the flash floods in Nepal,"},{"t":101.4,"txt":"it's 'clearly not over'."},{"t":103.4,"txt":"So this headline quotes Sky news reporter John Sparks, who says"},{"t":107.4,"txt":"that seven days after the floods in Nepal, it's 'clearly not over'."},{"t":111.9,"txt":"Let's look at the meaning of over."},{"t":113.6,"txt":"In this phrase, Phil, over has lots of meanings, doesn't it?"},{"t":117,"txt":"Yes, over often is used as a preposition."},{"t":120.4,"txt":"For example, the man walked over the bridge. Here,"},{"t":124.4,"txt":"though, the meaning is similar to finished."},{"t":127.4,"txt":"If something is not over, it's not finished."},{"t":130.8,"txt":"Yes. So a week has passed since the floods in Nepal,"},{"t":133.8,"txt":"but the consequences of the floods, the destruction and the loss of life,"},{"t":137.8,"txt":"are not over."},{"t":138.9,"txt":"They haven't ended yet."},{"t":140.3,"txt":"Rescuers are still searching for survivors, and many people need help."},{"t":144.6,"txt":"Now, of course, this is a really serious context to use over with this meaning,"},{"t":148.8,"txt":"but you can use it in a variety of situations."},{"t":152.4,"txt":"Yeah. So for example it's September now, so summer in the UK is over."},{"t":157.6,"txt":"People also often use it to describe relationships which have ended."},{"t":161.3,"txt":"It's over between them."},{"t":163,"txt":"A useful expression with the word over is over and done with."},{"t":168.2,"txt":"This means something is finished and no longer needs your attention."},{"t":172.3,"txt":"It has a sense of relief."},{"t":173.6,"txt":"For example, I'm so glad exam season is over and done with."},{"t":178.8,"txt":"Yes, it's also something we say when we want to stop procrastinating."},{"t":182.5,"txt":"For example, I know you don't want to clean your bedroom,"},{"t":184.8,"txt":"but come on, let's just get it over and done with."},{"t":191.6,"txt":"We had not over:"},{"t":193.8,"txt":"not finished."},{"t":197.3,"txt":"For example."},{"t":198.4,"txt":"Firefighters are working to put out the wildfires."},{"t":201.3,"txt":"But it's not over yet."},{"t":202.9,"txt":"The fires are still spreading."},{"t":208.6,"txt":"This is Learning English"},{"t":210,"txt":"from the News, our podcast about the news headlines. Today"},{"t":213.9,"txt":"we're talking about what's happened since the flash floods"},{"t":217.5,"txt":"in Nepal last week."},{"t":219.3,"txt":"Flash floods and landslides on the Nepal-Tibet border"},{"t":222.3,"txt":"have caused widespread damage, leaving more than 1000 dead"},{"t":226,"txt":"and thousands more missing."},{"t":227.5,"txt":"But what caused it?"},{"t":228.9,"txt":"Initially, many believed that an earthquake had caused a landslide,"},{"t":233,"txt":"which then caused the devastating floods."},{"t":235.5,"txt":"However, the US Geological Survey later confirmed"},{"t":239,"txt":"that the disaster was caused by a glacial collapse when a glacier"},{"t":243,"txt":"or part of a glacier breaks up."},{"t":245.2,"txt":"China has warned that there's a newly formed lake near the Nepal-Tibet border,"},{"t":250.6,"txt":"which could cause a second flooding."},{"t":253.2,"txt":"But experts have told the BBC"},{"t":255.7,"txt":"that it's possible that the lake could drain out naturally over time."},{"t":259.8,"txt":"Nepalese Prime Minister Belinda Shah said this incident indicates"},{"t":264,"txt":"that the risks we face in the Himalayan region are increasing"},{"t":267.7,"txt":"alongside climate change."},{"t":269.5,"txt":"Let's have another headline."},{"t":271.4,"txt":"This is from The Independent, a British news site."},{"t":274.3,"txt":"What triggered Nepal flash floods. And why are experts worried"},{"t":278.6,"txt":"a second flood is 'imminent'?"},{"t":280.8,"txt":"And that headline, again from The Independent, a British news site."},{"t":284.7,"txt":"What triggered Nepal flash floods. And why are experts worried"},{"t":288.4,"txt":"a second flood is 'imminent'?"},{"t":290.4,"txt":"So this article is about the causes of the flash floods in Nepal"},{"t":294.8,"txt":"and asks whether a second flood is imminent."},{"t":298.2,"txt":"Imminent means likely to happen soon."},{"t":300.8,"txt":"But we're looking at the verb trigger here."},{"t":304,"txt":"What can you tell us about it, Georgie?"},{"t":305.8,"txt":"Yes. What triggered Nepal flash floods is the question in the headline. Here"},{"t":310.6,"txt":"trigger means cause something to start."},{"t":312.9,"txt":"It might help to think of the literal meaning of trigger."},{"t":316,"txt":"Yes, trigger as a noun is something you pull on a gun."},{"t":321.5,"txt":"You pull the trigger to set off the gun to shoot."},{"t":325,"txt":"And that's literal."},{"t":326.5,"txt":"Metaphorically, if you trigger something,"},{"t":329.6,"txt":"you cause it to start - a bit like pulling a trigger on a gun."},{"t":333.9,"txt":"So scientists now think that a glacial collapse triggered"},{"t":337.7,"txt":"the flash floods in Nepal."},{"t":339.4,"txt":"The glacial collapse was the trigger."},{"t":341.9,"txt":"Another example could be the financial scandal triggered the CEO's resignation."},{"t":347.7,"txt":"Yes, it caused him to resign."},{"t":353,"txt":"We've had trigger: cause."},{"t":355.3,"txt":"Something to start."},{"t":357.8,"txt":"For example, her offensive comment triggered an argument"},{"t":361,"txt":"between the two colleagues."},{"t":366.9,"txt":"This is Learning English from the News from BBC Learning English."},{"t":370.5,"txt":"We're talking about the aftermath of the flash floods in Nepal."},{"t":374.6,"txt":"As the search for survivors from last week's flash floods continues."},{"t":378.9,"txt":"The clean up operation has also begun."},{"t":381.8,"txt":"Locals are coming together to clean up hospitals and schools,"},{"t":385.1,"txt":"which are covered in mud and debris after the floods."},{"t":388.3,"txt":"Let's have our next headline."},{"t":389.8,"txt":"And this is from Bloomberg, an international news organization."},{"t":393.5,"txt":"Flood-hit Nepal unsure how to rebuild with landscape wiped out."},{"t":398.9,"txt":"And that headline again: Flood-hit Nepal unsure how to rebuild"},{"t":403.2,"txt":"with landscape wiped out."},{"t":404.8,"txt":"And that's from Bloomberg."},{"t":407,"txt":"This headline is about the aftermath of the floods"},{"t":409.9,"txt":"and how Nepal will recover after so much destruction."},{"t":413.8,"txt":"It says the landscape is wiped out."},{"t":417.3,"txt":"What does this phrasal verb wipe out mean?"},{"t":420.5,"txt":"Well, if something is wiped out, it is completely destroyed or removed,"},{"t":424.6,"txt":"often in quite a sudden way."},{"t":426.4,"txt":"If a landscape has been wiped out, it's been destroyed or changed."},{"t":430.1,"txt":"So many buildings, roads or parts of the land are not there in the same form."},{"t":434.8,"txt":"Wipe something out is often used to talk about environmental issues."},{"t":438.8,"txt":"For example, deforestation has wiped out many species' habitats."},{"t":443.8,"txt":"It's also commonly used to talk about extinction when an entire species dies."},{"t":448.6,"txt":"For example, most scientists believe an asteroid wiped out the dinosaurs."},{"t":453.6,"txt":"Wipe out can be used more generally to talk about mass destruction,"},{"t":457.4,"txt":"so we can also use it to talk about war, for example."},{"t":463.6,"txt":"We've had wipe out: completely destroy often in a sudden way."},{"t":469.8,"txt":"For example, the hurricane wiped out many of the homes along the coast."},{"t":475.6,"txt":"That's it for this episode of Learning English from the News."},{"t":478.7,"txt":"If you want to learn more English to talk about climate and the weather,"},{"t":483.2,"txt":"we have a brand new series called How We Talk About Weather,"},{"t":487.5,"txt":"and you can find the link for it in the notes under this programme."},{"t":491.3,"txt":"Goodbye for now. Bye."}],"words":[{"t":0.04,"w":"From"},{"t":0.04,"w":"BBC"},{"t